{"name":"Port Gore","hex":"#3b436c","fetched":"2026-10-12","looked":{"definitions":[{"term":"port","whole":false,"parts":[{"part":"Noun","definitions":["A place on the coast at which ships can shelter, or dock to load and unload cargo or passengers.","A town or city containing such a place, a port city."]},{"part":"Adjective","definitions":["Of or relating to port, the left-hand side of a vessel when facing the bow."]}],"link":"https://en.wiktionary.org/wiki/port"},{"term":"gore","whole":false,"parts":[{"part":"Noun","definitions":["Blood, especially that from a wound when thickened due to exposure to the air.","A gout or mass of such blood."]},{"part":"Verb","definitions":["To cover or smear with blood."]}],"link":"https://en.wiktionary.org/wiki/gore"}],"entries":[{"title":"Port Gore","blocks":[{"text":"Port Gore is a bay and natural harbour at the northern end of the Marlborough Sounds in New Zealand. It is close to the northern tip of the South Island, at the western end of Cook Strait. It is directly west of the entrance to Queen Charlotte Sound."}],"link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Port_Gore","wikidata":"Q7230650"},{"title":"Port","blocks":[{"text":"A port is a maritime facility comprising one or more wharves or loading areas, where ships load and discharge cargo and passengers. Although usually situated on a sea coast or estuary, ports can also be found far inland, such as Hamburg, Manchester and Duluth; these access the sea via rivers or canals. Because of their roles as ports of entry for immigrants as well as soldiers in wartime, many port cities have experienced dramatic multi-ethnic and multicultural changes throughout their histories."}],"image":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/a/af/Manhattan.jpg/330px-Manhattan.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Port","wikidata":"Q44782"}],"images":[{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/1/1e/La_Lan